<header><p class="enc-kicker">Term · script</p><h1 class="enc-title">Clear script</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1648</span> · Also known as Todo bichig</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Clear script (todo bichig) is an alphabet created in 1648 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirat</a> monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaya-pandita">Zaya Pandita</a>, adapted from the traditional Mongolian script to write the Oirat language more exactly. It added marks that distinguished vowels and consonants left ambiguous by the older script. It was used by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmyks">Kalmyks</a> for religious and secular writing, remaining in use among the Kalmyks into the 1920s and among some Oirats in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> today.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/clear-script.jpg" alt="Clear script" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was ㄏㄨㄤㄉㄧ at English Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Smp_kalmyk.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
